   3230  fbfc							; 2026-10-20 (chunk 4): the corridor pads are SPLIT across the banks, exactly
   3231  fbfc							; like the tail pads below. A bank0 caller runs `sta $1FFx` AT A; the next
   3232  fbfc							; opcode fetch (A+3) already comes from the TARGET bank, which keeps only the
   3233  fbfc							; `jmp` half. Nothing in bank1/bank2/bank3 calls a CallPad (only `jmp
   3234  fbfc							; ReturnPad`), so bank0's `jmp` halves and the target banks' `sta` halves were
   3235  fbfc							; dead bytes — packing the live halves reclaimed 70 B of code (bank0 −33,
   3236  fbfc							; bank1 −37). The whole block is still 0 push / 0 cycles: `sta` changes no
   3237  fbfc							; register, no flag, no stack byte.
   3238  fbfc							;   A=$FBFC sta $1FF7	 A+3=$FBFF -> bank1 jmp UpdateBombSound
   3239  fbfc							;   A=$FBFF sta $1FF7	 A+3=$FC02 -> bank1 jmp UpdateJetSound
   3240  fbfc							;   A=$FC02 sta $1FF7	 A+3=$FC05 -> bank1 jmp BombSndDrop
   3241  fbfc							;   A=$FC05 sta $1FF7	 A+3=$FC08 -> bank1 jmp BombSndExplode
   3242  fbfc							;   A=$FC08 RETIRED by C3 (the exit handlers read the conn table in bank2)
